PostgreSQL manages database security through the unified concept of **Roles**. A role can represent a single database user (with `LOGIN`) or a user group (with `NOLOGIN`).

### The Principle of Least Privilege (PoLP)
Users and applications should be granted only the minimum permissions required to perform their specific business task.

### Standard DCL Workflow
```sql
-- 1. Create a group role (no login)
CREATE ROLE analyst_group WITH NOLOGIN;

-- 2. Grant permissions to the group
GRANT USAGE ON SCHEMA public TO analyst_group;
GRANT SELECT ON ALL TABLES IN SCHEMA public TO analyst_group;

-- 3. Create individual user roles with passwords and assign them to group
CREATE ROLE alice WITH LOGIN PASSWORD 'SecurePass123!';
GRANT analyst_group TO alice;

-- 4. Automatically grant future tables
ALTER DEFAULT PRIVILEGES IN SCHEMA public GRANT SELECT ON TABLES TO analyst_group;

-- 5. Revoking privileges
REVOKE INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE ON ALL TABLES IN SCHEMA public FROM analyst_group;
```
